{{!
  `%f` on Exec is the whole point of this template. Tauri's built-in deb entry
  emits a bare `Exec=`, and a desktop entry without a field code is passed no
  arguments at all — so a `.mmd` double-clicked in a file manager would launch
  the app with an empty editor no matter what the Rust side does. `%f` (single
  local file) matches `handle_cli_file_open`, which takes the first argv entry
  naming an existing file.

  Categories is also set here because upstream leaves it empty, which is
  invalid per the desktop entry spec and makes some menu implementations drop
  the entry entirely. Exactly one main category (Graphics) — listing two makes
  the app appear twice in the menu, which desktop-file-validate flags.

  These are Handlebars comments, so none of this text reaches the installed
  file — keep it that way, a shipped .desktop should be pure key=value.
}}
[Desktop Entry]
Type=Application
Name={{name}}
Comment={{comment}}
Exec={{exec}} %f
Icon={{icon}}
Terminal=false
Categories=Graphics;2DGraphics;VectorGraphics;
MimeType=text/vnd.mermaid;text/plain;
StartupWMClass={{exec}}
